---
// Editor: the editor the panels serve today (workbench/src/wasm/editor.ts with the device host, built into the
// firmware by workbench/scripts/editor-device.mjs, served at / from flash). Pictures: screenshots of a real panel's
// editor, in public/editor/ (taken after each flash). The next editor (workbench/EDITOR-V1.md, the /wasm/editor-v1
// mock) comes after 1.0: Updates > What's next.
import Docs, { type Heading } from '../layouts/Docs.astro';
import { url } from '../lib/nav';

const headings: Heading[] = [
  { depth: 2, slug: 'open', text: 'Opening it' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'homes', text: 'Homes and their cards' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'screens', text: 'Screens' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'save', text: 'Saving' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'next', text: 'What is coming' },
];
---

<Docs edit="site/src/pages/editor.astro" title="Editor" description="The web page every Facet panel serves: arrange Home's cards and the screens, set each screen up, save to the panel." group="Use Facet" current={url('/editor/')} headings={headings}>
  <p class="lede">
    Every Facet panel serves its own editor. Open the panel's address in a browser to arrange its Homes and their
    cards, put the screens in order and set each one up, then save it to the panel. Nothing to install: the page comes
    from the panel itself, the same on both panels.
  </p>

  <h2 id="open">Opening it</h2>
  <p>
    Type the panel's address into a browser on the same network: <code>http://facet-</code> and the six characters on
    the panel's Set up screen, then <code>.local/</code>, for example <code>http://facet-1a2b3c.local/</code>. The top
    bar says which panel it is and whether it is online. Every page and screen is drawn by the panel's own renderer, so
    the previews look as they do on the glass. Dark, Light, Auto and the accent dots change how the previews are drawn
    in your browser, not the panel's own look.
  </p>

  <h2 id="homes">Homes and their cards</h2>
  <p>
    A panel has one Home to start with; add one per room if you like. Click a Home to edit it: click a card to change
    it, drag a card to move it (on a touch screen, hold it first, then drag). <strong>Add a card</strong> offers a light
    or switch, a media player, a sensor (read only), a page, or any entity. Each Home has a name and can be the start
    page. Its top right corner holds the header buttons: a shortcut before Settings (Settings or one of the screens),
    and what shows beside the time (nothing, or a running timer).
  </p>

  <h2 id="screens">Screens</h2>
  <p>
    The screens on the panel are listed in the order you swipe through them; drag one to move it, or use Alt and the
    arrow keys. <strong>Add screen</strong> picks one of the factory screens the panel does not have yet. Click a screen to see it large, choose whether
    its card is on Home, and set its options, such as which speaker Media follows or which calendars Calendar shows.
  </p>

  <h2 id="save">Saving</h2>
  <p>
    Nothing reaches the panel until you press <strong>Save</strong>. Undo and Redo step through your changes before then.
    Save sends everything in one step: the panel checks it, applies it and keeps it across restarts. If the panel
    refuses it, it says why and keeps what it had. The panel also keeps the version before, so a change can be rolled
    back.
  </p>

  <h2 id="next">What is coming</h2>
  <p>
    A new editor comes after 1.0: one list of screens with a switch each, the panel's settings and its Home Assistant
    link on the same page, and a review of every change, checked by the panel, before it is sent.
    <a href={url('/updates/#next')}>What's next</a> follows it.
  </p>
</Docs>
